// THE CI STEP THAT RUNS THE DECLARED-ENTRY CALL-SHAPE INSTRUMENT OVER A PR's CHANGED .dag.
//
// gunbc.design_ruling::ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth is the law this step lives under. It buys a
// check, so it pays for it in a bound, and the bound it pays in is the DIFF: it never walks the
// tree. The whole-tree reading of this instrument was measured, and a scope the size of main's
// tracked .dag is half an hour of resolve, which no job budget of ours carries. It reads what this
// run changed, plus what IMPORTS something this run changed -- and that second half is why the step
// exists at all rather than being a reviewer's habit: the defect this row is named for
// (gunb_ai_pages_workflow passing claim_run to a step that declares no such parameter, discovered
// by a 1391-second / 13.3 GiB CI resolve) was a CALLER NOBODY HAD TO EDIT. A check scoped to
// changed files alone cannot see it. A check scoped to changed files plus their reverse importers
// sees it in seconds, because the caller is in the scope even though no edit touched it.
//
// THE BASE IS THE EVENT'S OWN BASE SHA, NOT main. `git diff --name-only <base>...HEAD`, merge-base
// semantics, with <base> read from the event that carries it: github.event.pull_request.base.sha on
// a pull_request, github.event.merge_group.base_sha on a merge_group. The two alternatives and why
// they fail are the reason this is spelled out rather than left to convention. GITHUB_BASE_REF is a
// BRANCH NAME: it moves under the run, so a main that gains a commit mid-job changes what "the
// diff" means, and two steps of one job would then disagree about the same PR. A two-dot diff
// against main counts main's own new commits as yours, so a PR that changed no .dag at all would be
// charged for someone else's -- and, worse, charged for THEIR break. The event's base sha is the
// commit this run was asked to judge, and it is the only thing that cannot move underneath it.
//
// THE BASE IS FETCHED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. The checkout here is full-depth today, but the step does
// not read that fact: an absent ancestor treated as "nothing changed" is the one silent arm this
// step has, so an unresolvable base is refused out loud. Fetching explicitly also means the step
// keeps its answer if the checkout's depth is ever tuned for speed by someone with other things on
// their mind.
//
// ONLY THE DIFF-CARRYING EVENTS ARE POLICED, and that is a condition on the step rather than an
// arm inside it. A push, a schedule, a dispatch or a workflow_call run carries no base commit: there
// is no PR, and so nothing this run changed that some other run has not already judged -- main's
// own tree is judged by the merge_group runs that produced it. Giving those events an empty diff and
// calling it a pass would be the silent green this row exists to retire; refusing them would make
// every scheduled run red for a reason nobody can act on. A step that does not run, with its
// condition stated, is the honest third thing.
//
// THE STEP AND THE INSTRUMENT SPLIT THE "NOTHING TO DO" CASE, deliberately. The STEP skips on an
// empty diff -- no .dag moved, so nothing this run could have broken -- and prints that it skipped,
// because a step that exits silently on the common path reads as a step that did not run. The
// INSTRUMENT, handed a non-empty scope, refuses a file it cannot read rather than skipping it (the
// header of dag/gunbc/instruments/declared_entry_call_shape.dag carries that argument). The step's
// skip is a judgement about a DIFF, which the step owns; the instrument's skip would be a judgement
// about a FILE it was handed, which it does not.
//
// THE REVERSE-IMPORTER SET COMES FROM git grep -l -F OVER TRACKED .dag AND MAY ONLY OVER-INCLUDE.
// The index answers "which files mention one of these module names", and mention is not import: a
// file that names the module in a comment is a candidate. That is tolerable only because the
// INSTRUMENT, not the index, is the authority on membership -- every candidate is re-read and its
// own import lines re-parsed by the instrument's one parser, and a file that merely mentions the
// stem is filtered there. Narrowing is an optimisation allowed to be wrong in ONE direction: an
// importer the index missed would be a missed refusal, so the stem search is the widest reading of
// the name (a fixed-string match anywhere in the file) and the instrument does the narrowing.
//
// THE CEILING IS A MIS-SCOPE DETECTOR, NOT A PERFORMANCE TUNING. `git diff <base>...HEAD` answered
// with a scope this large means the base is not the ancestor anyone thinks it is -- a squash-merge
// history, a base that resolved to a distant commit -- and at that point the step is not judging
// this run. It refuses, saying so, rather than spending an hour of a 90-minute job on a diff nobody
// asked for. There is deliberately NO FLOOR: a legitimate PR can change one leaf file that nothing
// imports, and a check that reds that PR has bought a false alarm with the safety it sold. The
// instrument's own refusal of an empty scope is the only bottom the step needs.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DECIDE, AND WHY THE INVOCATION ARRIVES BY PARAMETER. The binary this job
// runs, the source-root pair it runs it with, and the memory envelope it runs inside belong to the
// job that embeds the step (gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow), which owns those three facts
// elsewhere too; taking them as parameters keeps this file the authority on the DIFF and nothing
// else, and it keeps the lane that witnesses this file resolvable without the CI hub behind those
// facts -- a first draft that imported them here made one claim of this lane take 13 min 54 s to
// resolve, which is the budget law applied to the check that exists to enforce it.
//
// WHY THE SHELL'S VARIABLE REFERENCES ARE ASSEMBLED AND NOT TYPED. The string medium reads `${name}`
// as a reference to a value in scope, so a shell variable written literally is a reference to a
// variable this module does not declare -- and the resolver is right to refuse it. sh() builds the
// reference instead, so the emitted script is the same shell and the .dag stays a description of it.

module gunbc.witness.declared_entry_call_shape_step

import std.types { List, String }
import std.optional { Present }
import extdeps.github.actions { Step, RunStep }
import v2.cli.parse_verdict_invocation {
  ParseVerdictInvocationArg,
  parse_verdict_invocation_command,
  parse_verdict_invocation_source_root_args
}

// The step's identity. Read by the job's bound-step list and pinned by the lane witness, so this is
// the one spelling of both.
data declared_entry_call_shape_step_name: String = "Declared entry call shape"

data declared_entry_call_shape_step_id: String = "declared-entry-call-shape"

// The instrument this step runs. The step names the FILE and the ENTRY FUNCTION; the instrument
// module owns what it does with them.
data declared_entry_call_shape_instrument_entry: String =
  "dag/gunbc/instruments/declared_entry_call_shape.dag"

data declared_entry_call_shape_entry_function: String = "main"

// The scope files, spelled once each. They are written under `mktemp -d` rather than into the checkout:
// the step is a read of the diff, not a producer, and a step that leaves untracked files in the tree
// makes every later `git status` in the job a question about someone else's artefact. The names are
// consts because the instrument is handed the same two paths the step wrote, and a mismatch between
// what is written and what is passed is a check that silently reads nothing.
data declared_entry_call_shape_changed_name: String = "changed.txt"

data declared_entry_call_shape_candidate_name: String = "candidates.txt"

data declared_entry_call_shape_stems_name: String = "stems.txt"

// Above this many .dag files in scope the step is not judging this run any more. Spelled as text
// because it is interpolated into the shell test, and a number in a shell test is text whatever the
// language calls it here.
data declared_entry_call_shape_scope_ceiling: String = "1200"

// The event's base commit, as the one expression that carries it. `||` is GitHub's fallback: a
// pull_request event has no merge_group payload and a merge_group event has no pull_request payload,
// so exactly one arm resolves on either event and neither on any other -- which is how the step
// learns it is on an event with no diff to police.
data declared_entry_base_sha_expression: String =
  "${{ github.event.pull_request.base.sha || github.event.merge_group.base_sha }}"

// The events this step runs on, stated as the step's condition. See the header: the alternative to
// a condition is a silent green on the events that carry no base.
data declared_entry_call_shape_if_condition: String =
  "github.event_name == 'pull_request' || github.event_name == 'merge_group'"

// The CLI verb and the option tokens this invocation passes. These are the spellings the parser in
// v1_compiler.cli_run reads; they are repeated here because 260 .dag files already spell them and no
// module owns them, and minting a private authority for one consumer of a 260-consumer spelling is a
// worse fork than the copy.
data declared_entry_call_shape_verb: String = "run"

data declared_entry_option_entry: String = "--entry"

data declared_entry_option_function: String = "--function"

data declared_entry_option_arg: String = "--arg"

data declared_entry_changed_arg: String = "changed_paths_file"

data declared_entry_candidates_arg: String = "candidate_importers_file"

// A shell variable reference, assembled. See the header.
fn sh(name: String) -> String {
  join(["$", "{", name, "}"], "")
}

// The two paths exactly as the shell will build them, so the argv and the writes cannot disagree.
data declared_entry_changed_path: String =
  join(["/", declared_entry_call_shape_changed_name], "")

data declared_entry_candidate_path: String =
  join(["/", declared_entry_call_shape_candidate_name], "")

// A brace-wrapped word, assembled for the same reason: the medium reads a bare {name} as a reference too,
// and `git cat-file -e $base^{commit}` is the git idiom worth keeping whole.
fn braces(name: String) -> String {
  join(["{", name, "}"], "")
}

// THE INSTRUMENT'S INVOCATION, rendered through the one CLI-invocation authority so the option
// sequence this step passes is rendered the same way every other emitted gunbc invocation is.
fn declared_entry_call_shape_command(
  bin: String,
  source_roots: List<String>
) -> String {
  parse_verdict_invocation_command(
    bin: bin,
    verb: declared_entry_call_shape_verb,
    args: concat(
      parse_verdict_invocation_source_root_args(roots: source_roots),
      [
        ParseVerdictInvocationArg { option: declared_entry_option_entry, value: declared_entry_call_shape_instrument_entry },
        ParseVerdictInvocationArg { option: declared_entry_option_function, value: declared_entry_call_shape_entry_function },
        ParseVerdictInvocationArg {
          option: declared_entry_option_arg,
          // The path is the shell's, not a literal: the temp directory is created when the step runs, so
          // the only correct way to name the file the step wrote is to reference the variable that holds
          // it. A const path here would be an absolute path to a file that never exists, and the
          // instrument would be handed a scope of nothing -- the claim
          // the_step_hands_the_instrument_its_scope_files is what catches that, and it did.
          value: join([declared_entry_changed_arg, "=", sh("dir"), declared_entry_changed_path], "")
        },
        ParseVerdictInvocationArg {
          option: declared_entry_option_arg,
          value: join([declared_entry_candidates_arg, "=", sh("dir"), declared_entry_candidate_path], "")
        }
      ]
    )
  )
}

// THE SCOPE, as shell lines. Each line is one fact the step asserts about the diff, in the order it
// must be true: resolve the base, refuse without it, take the changed set, skip if empty, widen to
// reverse importers, refuse a scope that cannot be this run's, and only then name the scope it is
// handing the instrument.
fn declared_entry_scope_lines() -> List<String> {
  [
    "set -euo pipefail",
    join(["base='", declared_entry_base_sha_expression, "'"], ""),
    join(["if [ -z \"", sh("base"), "\" ]; then"], ""),
    "  echo 'skipped: this event carries no base commit to diff against, so this run changed nothing for the check to judge'",
    "  exit 0",
    "fi",
    "dir=$(mktemp -d)",
    join(["changed=\"", sh("dir"), "/", declared_entry_call_shape_changed_name, "\""], ""),
    join(["candidates=\"", sh("dir"), "/", declared_entry_call_shape_candidate_name, "\""], ""),
    // Present in the full-depth checkout already, so the fetch is a no-op there; it is the step's
    // answer for a checkout that is not, and the refusal is the answer for a base that is simply
    // not there. Neither is allowed to become a green.
    join([
      "git cat-file -e \"", sh("base"), "^", braces("commit"), "\" 2>/dev/null || git fetch --no-tags --depth=1 origin \"", sh("base"), "\" >/dev/null 2>&1 || {"
    ], ""),
    join([
      "  echo \"the event base commit ", sh("base"), " is not in this checkout and could not be fetched: refusing to guess a scope\" 1>&2"
    ], ""),
    "  exit 1",
    "}",
    join([
      "git diff --name-only \"", sh("base"), "...HEAD\" -- '*.dag' > \"", sh("changed"), "\""
    ], ""),
    join(["if [ ! -s \"", sh("changed"), "\" ]; then"], ""),
    "  echo 'skipped: no .dag change in this diff'",
    "  exit 0",
    "fi",
    join(["stems=\"", sh("dir"), "/", declared_entry_call_shape_stems_name, "\""], ""),
    // A changed file's MODULE NAME is what an importer writes, so the stem is the path with its
    // source root and extension removed and its separators dotted. A path outside either root keeps
    // its first directory: an odd stem over-includes candidates, which the instrument narrows, and
    // an over-narrow stem would lose an importer, which is the direction that must not be reachable.
    join([
      "sed -E 's|^dag/||; s|^src/v2/||; s|\\.dag$||; s|/|.|g' \"", sh("changed"), "\" | sed '/^$/d' > \"", sh("stems"), "\""
    ], ""),
    join([": > \"", sh("candidates"), "\""], ""),
    join(["if [ -s \"", sh("stems"), "\" ]; then"], ""),
    // git grep exits 1 on 'no match', which is a normal answer here, and >=2 on a real error, which
    // is not: the two are told apart rather than swallowed together.
    join([
      "  rc=0; git grep -l -F -f \"", sh("stems"), "\" -- '*.dag' > \"", sh("candidates"), ".grep\" || rc=$?"
    ], ""),
    join(["  if [ \"", sh("rc"), "\" -gt 1 ]; then exit \"", sh("rc"), "\"; fi"], ""),
    join(["  cat \"", sh("candidates"), ".grep\" >> \"", sh("candidates"), "\""], ""),
    "fi",
    join(["sort -u \"", sh("changed"), "\" \"", sh("candidates"), "\" -o \"", sh("candidates"), "\""], ""),
    join(["scope=$(wc -l < \"", sh("candidates"), "\" | tr -d ' ')"], ""),
    join(["if [ \"", sh("scope"), "\" -gt ", declared_entry_call_shape_scope_ceiling, " ]; then"], ""),
    join([
      "  echo \"scope of ", sh("scope"), " .dag files is above the ceiling of ", declared_entry_call_shape_scope_ceiling, ": this is not this run's diff, and the step refuses to judge it\" 1>&2"
    ], ""),
    "  exit 1",
    "fi",
    // The scope the step actually computed, printed before the instrument speaks: the number the
    // instrument's own counts line will be read against, and the one line that makes a mis-scoped
    // run visible in the log rather than merely green.
    join([
      "echo \"scope: ", sh("scope"), " .dag files (changed plus reverse importers, diffed against ", sh("base"), ")\""
    ], "")
  ]
}

// THE STEP. Unguarded on its two events -- a check behind a cache-reuse condition is the extdeps-gate
// defect this row exists to retire: enrolled, green, and never actually running. The gunbc binary,
// the source-root pair and the memory envelope come from the job that embeds it, so the resolve is
// accounted in the job's cgroup like the walks beside it.
fn declared_entry_call_shape_step(
  bin: String,
  source_roots: List<String>
) -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: declared_entry_call_shape_step_name },
    id: Present { value: declared_entry_call_shape_step_id },
    run: join(concat(declared_entry_scope_lines(), [declared_entry_call_shape_command(bin: bin, source_roots: source_roots)]), "\n"),
    shell: none,
    // NO ENVELOPE INPUT, ON PURPOSE. The envelope requirement this job sets on its subject steps
    // (GUNBC_MEMORY_ENVELOPE_REQUIRED=slot) is read by the TEST route -- v1_compiler.cli_run.target_invocation_host
    // test_verb_checked, the same file that declares MEMORY_ENVELOPE_REQUIRED_ENV -- and not by `gunbc run`,
    // which is what this step invokes. Carrying the input would put a line in the shipped YAML asserting a
    // bound the verb never takes, which is a receipt for a measurement nobody made. What bounds this step is
    // its scope, and what it costs is measured: the instrument resolves 53 modules in about 5 s at a peak of
    // roughly 176 MiB, and the scan of a 54-file scope runs in about 24 s. See the design note
    // handoff/2026-11-26/gunbc-entries-resolve-checked-in-ci-design.md.
    env: none,
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: declared_entry_call_shape_if_condition },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}
